  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126329,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 349,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T08:22:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126330,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 349,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The best method of sampling to observe diagnostic features is a full-thickness longitudinal nail unit excision encompassing the lesion, which is also sectioned or oriented in this plane for processing {29729116}. Diagnostic features may remain attached to the nail plate during nail plate avulsion or reflection, which also often avulses the superficial nail unit epithelium. Proximally, there may be slight hyperplasia of nail matrix epithelium, and so-called matrical metaplasia of the nail bed, consisting of a pattern of maturation similar to matrix, with retained keratinocyte nuclei over eosinophilic superficial keratinocytes. More distally, there is nail bed hyperplasia (which is often papillated most distally) with hypergranulosis, mimicking verruca vulgaris, which is the main entity in the differential diagnosis. Some cases demonstrate multinucleate keratinocytes in the mid to distal nail bed as well, giving rise to the original name for this entity {7669645; 10886147; 26518173}. Subungual haemorrhage may be present. Distal subungual parakeratosis and hyperkeratosis is characteristic, and it may indent the overlying nail plate undersurface. These features, as well as haemorrhage, can be seen in nail plate clippings {26518173}. A single case of malignant onychopapilloma has been described, with histological features that overlap with those of squamous cell carcinoma in&nbsp;situ {33067854}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T08:22:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
